        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "id": "explain-text",
          "fp": "46845204",
          "text": "Three. It costs a little on every call, so measure it against a real workload, keep the counters where every copy of the service can read them, and decide in advance what the caller gets back while the gate is shut. Without it each caller waits out its full timeout, the waiting piles up behind the slow part, and the healthy parts starve for connections that never come back. Teams that skip this step usually find out during the first real outage, at the worst hour.",
          "md": "Three. It costs a little on every call, so measure it against a real workload, keep the counters where every copy of the service can read them, and decide in advance what the caller gets back while the gate is shut. Without it each caller waits out its full timeout, the waiting piles up behind the slow part, and the healthy parts starve for connections that never come back. Teams that skip this step usually find out during the first real outage, at the worst hour."
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "id": null,
          "fp": null,
          "ordered": false,
          "start": null,
          "items": [
            {
              "id": "explain-li-1",
              "fp": "1cf782f5",
